  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x]^{\u2032}}{{[{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sec}^{2}\u2061x}{{sec}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}\u2061x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
